<!--
    kuna `libcnames`, the address of a stack object: a local array, structure
    or scalar whose own address a library call takes keeps a `vN` name
    although the library says what the object is: `read(a0,v2,0x100)`,
    `stat(a0,&v1)`, `ReadFile(hFile,v3,0x200,&v2,NULL)`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P6 "naming-policy".  With the option on, the
    pointer parameter's name names the object it points at, through every
    reference to it: glibc's names as they are (`buf`, `stat_loc`, `endptr`,
    `tv`), a Windows SDK name less its `lp`/`p` pointer prefix (`lpBuffer`
    names the buffer `Buffer`, `lpNumberOfBytesRead` the count
    `NumberOfBytesRead`).  A name that says only that the argument is a
    pointer (`fread`'s `ptr`) names nothing that is not itself a pointer, a
    call that only fills the object (`explicit_bzero`, `memset`) is no
    evidence, and a call to a function of the program is none either.  Like
    every library name it is a second name: the object keeps its number, and
    a directive reaches it by either name.

    The x86-64 fixture is
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/libcnames_addr_x86_64
    (gcc 11 -O2 -fno-inline, source and recipe in libcnames_addr.c); the
    Windows one is libcnames_win32_i386.exe (see libcnames_win32.c).

    Pass 1 sets the option off: `echo`'s buffer is `v2` and the Windows
    copy loop's buffer and counts are `v3`, `v2` and `v4`.  Pass 2 turns it
    back on, the shipped default: `buf` for read's buffer and stat's
    structure, `stat_loc` for waitpid's status, `endptr` for strtol's end
    pointer, `tv` for the timeval explicit_bzero clears before
    gettimeofday fills it; fread's structure and the array passed to the
    program's own `keep` keep `v1`, and so does `reuse`'s int, which read
    takes as its `buf` and waitpid then as its `stat_loc`: a scalar named
    two ways is two values its stack slot holds in turn.  On Windows the
    buffer is `Buffer` and
    the counts `NumberOfBytesRead` and `NumberOfBytesWritten`.  Then two
    console renames against `echo`: `buf` to `block` leaves `n` as it was,
    and `v2` to `chunk` reaches the same buffer by its number.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
